\fs32\b Async and Await: A Beginner's Guide\b0\fs24\par
\par
\b What are async and await?\b0\par
JavaScript often performs tasks that take time, such as opening a webpage, reading a file, calling an API, or loading an image. These tasks usually return a Promise. A Promise is like a receipt that says: "I will give you the result later."\par
\par
\b The async keyword\b0\par
The async keyword marks a function as one that may perform asynchronous work. An async function always returns a Promise.\par
\par
\f1 async function sayHello() {\line
  return "Hello";\line
}\f0\par
\par
Even though the function returns a string, JavaScript wraps that result in a Promise.\par
\par
\b The await keyword\b0\par
The await keyword means: "Wait for this Promise to finish, then give me its result." It normally appears inside an async function.\par
\par
\f1 async function getMessage() {\line
  const message = await sayHello();\line
  console.log(message);\line
}\line
\line
getMessage();\f0\par
\par
The waiting happens inside getMessage; it does not freeze the entire JavaScript program.\par
\par
\b Playwright example\b0\par
\f1 test('homepage title', async ({ page }) => {\line
  await page.goto('https://playwright.dev/');\line
  await expect(page).toHaveTitle(\line
    'Fast and reliable end-to-end testing for modern web apps | Playwright'\line
  );\line
});\f0\par
\par
async means the test contains actions that take time. await page.goto means wait until navigation finishes. await expect means wait while Playwright checks the title.\par
\par
\b When should you use async and await?\b0\par
Use them when working with a Promise:\par
\f1 const response = await fetch('https://example.com');\line
const data = await response.json();\line
await page.click('button');\line
await page.goto('https://example.com');\f0\par
\par
You do not need them for immediate calculations:\par
\f1 const sum = 2 + 3;\line
const name = 'Rohan';\line
console.log(name);\f0\par
\par
\b Sequential work\b0\par
Use separate await statements when the second action depends on the first:\par
\f1 const response = await fetch('/user');\line
const user = await response.json();\line
console.log(user.name);\f0\par
\par
In Playwright, login steps usually need to happen in order:\par
\f1 await page.goto('/login');\line
await page.fill('#username', 'rohan');\line
await page.click('#submit');\f0\par
\par
\b Parallel work\b0\par
If tasks do not depend on each other, run them together with Promise.all. This can be faster:\par
\f1 const [users, products] = await Promise.all([\line
  fetch('/users'),\line
  fetch('/products')\line
]);\f0\par
\par
\b Handling errors\b0\par
Use try and catch when asynchronous work can fail:\par
\f1 async function loadPage() {\line
  try {\line
    const response = await fetch('https://example.com');\line
    if (!response.ok) {\line
      throw new Error('Request failed');\line
    }\line
    return await response.text();\line
  } catch (error) {\line
    console.log('Something went wrong:', error);\line
  }\line
}\f0\par
\par
\b Common mistakes\b0\par
1. Forgetting await: const data = fetch('/users') gives you a Promise, not the actual data. Use const data = await fetch('/users').\par
2. Using await outside an async function: add async to the containing function.\par
3. Waiting unnecessarily: use Promise.all for independent tasks instead of waiting for each one sequentially.\par
\par
\b Easy rule to remember\b0\par
Use async on a function that performs waiting work. Use await before a Promise when you need its result. Use multiple await statements when steps must happen in order. Use Promise.all when independent tasks can happen at the same time. Use try and catch when an operation can fail.\par
\par
\b A complete Playwright pattern\b0\par
\f1 test('my test', async ({ page }) => {\line
  await page.goto('https://example.com');\line
  await page.click('button');\line
  await expect(page).toHaveTitle('Example');\line
});\f0\par
}